    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Our 200 liver biopsies were sequenced in four batches, and every biopsy has a numeric fibrosis score; I have a table of these values per sample and about 15,000 genes measured. To plan follow-up experiments, I want to see which gene pairs move together because of fibrosis and which pairing is just down to batch."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "What I understood you are asking for:\n1. \"I want to see which gene pairs move together because of fibrosis and which pairing is just down to batch.\" -- available from COBRA.\n\n**COBRA**. COBRA models co-expression as a function of sample covariates through conditional covariance estimation. Correcting gene means alone may leave covariance confounding. Covariate-associated components are not TF-to-gene regulatory edges or proof that a covariate causes the expression pattern.\n\nYou would need expression matrix, sample covariate design matrix. The analysis would provide inferred gene-to-gene associations.\n\nNo files were inspected and no analysis ran."
    }
  ],
